WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.776
Welcome to Week Six of CS104: Relations.

2
00:00:03.776 --> 00:00:10.250
Last week, Cartesian products gave us ordered pairs, and functions assigned exactly

3
00:00:10.250 --> 00:00:12.947
one output to each input.

4
00:00:12.947 --> 00:00:19.421
A relation is more general: it records whichever pairs satisfy a chosen

5
00:00:19.421 --> 00:00:19.961
condition.

6
00:00:19.961 --> 00:00:26.434
We will describe relations, test their properties, and study equivalence relations that

7
00:00:26.434 --> 00:00:28.592
group objects into classes.

8
00:00:28.592 --> 00:00:35.066
We finish by using congruence classes to justify modular arithmetic and the

9
00:00:35.066 --> 00:00:41.000
familiar divisibility test based on the sum of a number's digits.

10
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:43.945
Our route has three sections.

11
00:00:43.945 --> 00:00:51.014
First, we represent relations with statements, ordered pairs, and arrow diagrams, and

12
00:00:51.014 --> 00:00:53.959
identify their domains and ranges.

13
00:00:53.959 --> 00:01:00.438
Second, we test reflexivity, symmetry, and transitivity, using proofs and counterexamples.

14
00:01:00.438 --> 00:01:06.329
A relation with all three properties is an equivalence relation.

15
00:01:06.329 --> 00:01:11.630
Third, we show how equivalence classes partition a set.

16
00:01:11.630 --> 00:01:18.699
Congruence modulo a positive integer supplies a central example, where infinitely many

17
00:01:18.699 --> 00:01:24.000
integers can be handled through finitely many remainder classes.

18
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:28.203
We begin with the definition of a relation.

19
00:01:28.203 --> 00:01:34.508
Think of two sets of possible objects and a condition that connects

20
00:01:34.508 --> 00:01:36.085
certain ordered pairs.

21
00:01:36.085 --> 00:01:42.390
Unlike a function, a relation may leave some objects unconnected or connect

22
00:01:42.390 --> 00:01:45.017
one object to several others.

23
00:01:45.017 --> 00:01:51.322
Our job is to specify exactly which pairs occur, without accidentally imposing

24
00:01:51.322 --> 00:01:55.000
the extra requirements that belong to functions.

25
00:01:55.000 --> 00:02:00.806
The first example relates zero, one, and two to numbers from zero

26
00:02:00.806 --> 00:02:02.742
through four by divisibility.

27
00:02:02.742 --> 00:02:08.548
Under our definition, a divides b when b equals a times an

28
00:02:08.548 --> 00:02:09.032
integer.

29
00:02:09.032 --> 00:02:14.839
Thus zero divides zero, because zero equals zero times an integer, but

30
00:02:14.839 --> 00:02:17.258
zero divides no nonzero integer.

31
00:02:17.258 --> 00:02:23.065
One divides every integer, and two divides zero, two, and four in

32
00:02:23.065 --> 00:02:24.516
the target set.

33
00:02:24.516 --> 00:02:28.387
Divisibility here does not require dividing by zero.

34
00:02:28.387 --> 00:02:34.194
The second example records father and son pairs: Tom is paired with

35
00:02:34.194 --> 00:02:40.000
Alan and Bob, Dick with Carl, and Harry has no listed connection.

36
00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:44.966
If R is a relation from A to B, we write a

37
00:02:44.966 --> 00:02:49.103
R b to mean that a is related to b.

38
00:02:49.103 --> 00:02:54.069
This is another way to write that the ordered pair a, b

39
00:02:54.069 --> 00:02:55.310
belongs to R.

40
00:02:55.310 --> 00:03:00.276
The order carries meaning: Tom is the father of Alan does not

41
00:03:00.276 --> 00:03:03.586
assert that Alan is the father of Tom.

42
00:03:03.586 --> 00:03:06.069
Familiar relations often have familiar symbols.

43
00:03:06.069 --> 00:03:11.034
For divisibility we use the vertical bar instead of R, so two

44
00:03:11.034 --> 00:03:16.000
divides four is a relation statement about the ordered pair two, four.

45
00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:20.770
An arrow diagram draws one arrow for every related pair.

46
00:03:20.770 --> 00:03:26.017
For the divisibility example, zero has an arrow only to zero.

47
00:03:26.017 --> 00:03:29.833
One has arrows to all five target values.

48
00:03:29.833 --> 00:03:33.649
Two has arrows to zero, two, and four.

49
00:03:33.649 --> 00:03:36.511
That gives nine arrows in total.

50
00:03:36.511 --> 00:03:42.236
Multiple arrows may leave one source, and several sources may point to

51
00:03:42.236 --> 00:03:43.667
the same target.

52
00:03:43.667 --> 00:03:47.483
Neither feature is a problem for a relation.

53
00:03:47.483 --> 00:03:53.207
To check that the diagram is complete, test each candidate pair against

54
00:03:53.207 --> 00:03:57.500
the condition rather than guessing arrows from the picture.

55
00:03:57.500 --> 00:04:02.700
Formally, a relation from A to B is any subset of the

56
00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:04.867
Cartesian product A times B.

57
00:04:04.867 --> 00:04:10.067
Each pair must have its first coordinate in A and second in

58
00:04:10.067 --> 00:04:10.500
B.

59
00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:15.700
The displayed relation contains a, zero; b, two; c, four; and a,

60
00:04:15.700 --> 00:04:16.133
two.

61
00:04:16.133 --> 00:04:19.167
Repeating the first coordinate a is allowed.

62
00:04:19.167 --> 00:04:24.367
If both coordinates are chosen from the same set, we call it

63
00:04:24.367 --> 00:04:26.533
a relation on that set.

64
00:04:26.533 --> 00:04:31.733
The example T is a relation on B because all its coordinates

65
00:04:31.733 --> 00:04:36.500
belong to B, even though some elements of B never appear.

66
00:04:36.500 --> 00:04:39.185
Many mathematical statements define relations.

67
00:04:39.185 --> 00:04:45.630
Less than and equality give subsets of the real plane by selecting

68
00:04:45.630 --> 00:04:51.000
pairs satisfying x less than y or x equals y.

69
00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:56.907
Divisibility selects integer pairs m, n for which m divides n.

70
00:04:56.907 --> 00:05:03.352
Congruence modulo a fixed positive integer selects integer pairs whose difference is

71
00:05:03.352 --> 00:05:05.500
divisible by that modulus.

72
00:05:05.500 --> 00:05:10.870
Each description combines an underlying Cartesian product with a predicate.

73
00:05:10.870 --> 00:05:17.315
Keep the domains and any fixed parameter visible; changing them can change

74
00:05:17.315 --> 00:05:20.000
the properties of the relation.

75
00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:25.586
The domain of a relation contains the first coordinates that actually occur,

76
00:05:25.586 --> 00:05:30.241
and the range contains the second coordinates that actually occur.

77
00:05:30.241 --> 00:05:35.828
In the example R contains one, a; one, b; and three, a.

78
00:05:35.828 --> 00:05:41.414
Its domain is one and three, and its range is a and

79
00:05:41.414 --> 00:05:41.879
b.

80
00:05:41.879 --> 00:05:47.466
The ambient source set also contains two, but two has no outgoing

81
00:05:47.466 --> 00:05:51.190
arrow and is absent from the relation's domain.

82
00:05:51.190 --> 00:05:54.448
Similarly c is absent from the range.

83
00:05:54.448 --> 00:06:00.034
Membership in either projection requires at least one partner in the other

84
00:06:00.034 --> 00:06:00.500
set.

85
00:06:00.500 --> 00:06:06.191
Consider the relation defined by four x squared plus y squared equals

86
00:06:06.191 --> 00:06:06.665
sixteen.

87
00:06:06.665 --> 00:06:09.036
Its graph is an ellipse.

88
00:06:09.036 --> 00:06:14.727
For a real y to exist, sixteen minus four x squared must

89
00:06:14.727 --> 00:06:19.469
be nonnegative, so x lies between negative two and two.

90
00:06:19.469 --> 00:06:25.160
Every such x has a real y, for example the nonnegative square

91
00:06:25.160 --> 00:06:28.479
root of sixteen minus four x squared.

92
00:06:28.479 --> 00:06:34.170
Therefore the domain is the full closed interval from negative two to

93
00:06:34.170 --> 00:06:34.644
two.

94
00:06:34.644 --> 00:06:39.861
Similarly the range is negative four through four, including the endpoints.

95
00:06:39.861 --> 00:06:45.552
A relation's domain and range are projections of its graph onto the

96
00:06:45.552 --> 00:06:46.500
coordinate axes.

97
00:06:46.500 --> 00:06:53.271
Section six point two studies properties of a relation on one set

98
00:06:53.271 --> 00:06:53.836
A.

99
00:06:53.836 --> 00:07:00.607
We ask whether every element relates to itself, whether every connection can

100
00:07:00.607 --> 00:07:06.814
be reversed, and whether two successive connections require a direct one.

101
00:07:06.814 --> 00:07:09.071
These are universal conditions.

102
00:07:09.071 --> 00:07:15.843
A proof must address arbitrary elements, while a disproof needs one carefully

103
00:07:15.843 --> 00:07:16.971
chosen violation.

104
00:07:16.971 --> 00:07:23.743
The underlying set matters especially for reflexivity, because every member must be

105
00:07:23.743 --> 00:07:26.000
checked, including isolated elements.

106
00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:31.327
Reflexive means x is related to itself for every x in A.

107
00:07:31.327 --> 00:07:35.321
In an arrow diagram, every vertex needs a loop.

108
00:07:35.321 --> 00:07:40.648
Symmetric means that whenever x is related to y, y is related

109
00:07:40.648 --> 00:07:41.536
to x.

110
00:07:41.536 --> 00:07:44.199
Every arrow therefore needs its reverse.

111
00:07:44.199 --> 00:07:49.526
Transitive means that whenever x is related to y and y to

112
00:07:49.526 --> 00:07:52.189
z, x is related to z.

113
00:07:52.189 --> 00:07:55.740
Every two-step path needs the corresponding direct connection.

114
00:07:55.740 --> 00:07:58.847
The quantified variables need not be distinct.

115
00:07:58.847 --> 00:08:04.173
In particular, a path from x to y and back to x

116
00:08:04.173 --> 00:08:09.500
requires the loop from x to itself if the relation is transitive.

117
00:08:09.500 --> 00:08:14.758
Divisibility on the integers is reflexive because a equals a times one,

118
00:08:14.758 --> 00:08:16.949
so every a divides itself.

119
00:08:16.949 --> 00:08:22.208
It is not symmetric: one divides two, but two does not divide

120
00:08:22.208 --> 00:08:22.646
one.

121
00:08:22.646 --> 00:08:27.028
For transitivity, suppose a divides b and b divides c.

122
00:08:27.028 --> 00:08:32.287
Write b equals a k and c equals b l for integers

123
00:08:32.287 --> 00:08:33.601
k and l.

124
00:08:33.601 --> 00:08:37.107
Substituting gives c equals a times k l.

125
00:08:37.107 --> 00:08:41.051
Since k l is an integer, a divides c.

126
00:08:41.051 --> 00:08:46.309
This argument also handles zero because it uses multiplication rather than division

127
00:08:46.309 --> 00:08:48.500
by a possibly zero number.

128
00:08:48.500 --> 00:08:54.053
Define a relation on the rational numbers by declaring a related to

129
00:08:54.053 --> 00:08:57.755
b when a minus b is an integer.

130
00:08:57.755 --> 00:09:02.383
Reflexivity follows because a minus a equals zero, an integer.

131
00:09:02.383 --> 00:09:07.936
For symmetry, if a minus b is an integer, its negative b

132
00:09:07.936 --> 00:09:10.713
minus a is also an integer.

133
00:09:10.713 --> 00:09:16.266
For transitivity, if a minus b and b minus c are integers,

134
00:09:16.266 --> 00:09:19.968
their sum a minus c is an integer.

135
00:09:19.968 --> 00:09:25.521
Notice the repeated pattern: translate the relation into its defining condition, use

136
00:09:25.521 --> 00:09:31.074
a closure property of the integers, and translate back to the required

137
00:09:31.074 --> 00:09:32.000
relation statement.

138
00:09:32.000 --> 00:09:37.500
The claimed proof that symmetry and transitivity imply reflexivity is wrong.

139
00:09:37.500 --> 00:09:43.500
It begins by assuming that x is related to some y, but

140
00:09:43.500 --> 00:09:47.500
an arbitrary x may have no related partner.

141
00:09:47.500 --> 00:09:52.000
Symmetry and transitivity do not provide that missing existence.

142
00:09:52.000 --> 00:09:58.000
For a counterexample, take A containing one and two, and R containing

143
00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:00.500
only the pair one, one.

144
00:10:00.500 --> 00:10:06.500
It is symmetric and transitive, but it lacks the pair two, two,

145
00:10:06.500 --> 00:10:10.000
so it is not reflexive on A.

146
00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:16.000
The empty relation on a nonempty set is another counterexample: the conditional

147
00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:19.000
properties hold vacuously, while reflexivity fails.

148
00:10:19.000 --> 00:10:24.765
The table summarises familiar relations on their stated number systems.

149
00:10:24.765 --> 00:10:29.953
Less than is transitive, but not reflexive or symmetric.

150
00:10:29.953 --> 00:10:32.835
Equality has all three properties.

151
00:10:32.835 --> 00:10:37.447
Divisibility is reflexive and transitive, but not symmetric.

152
00:10:37.447 --> 00:10:42.059
Congruence modulo a positive integer has all three.

153
00:10:42.059 --> 00:10:48.976
For congruence, reflexivity uses a minus a equal to zero; symmetry uses

154
00:10:48.976 --> 00:10:55.894
the negative of a divisible difference; transitivity uses the sum of two

155
00:10:55.894 --> 00:10:57.047
divisible differences.

156
00:10:57.047 --> 00:11:03.965
A summary table should be supported by these definitions and short proofs,

157
00:11:03.965 --> 00:11:08.000
rather than memorised without the underlying reason.

158
00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:14.072
An equivalence relation is a relation on A that is reflexive, symmetric,

159
00:11:14.072 --> 00:11:15.084
and transitive.

160
00:11:15.084 --> 00:11:17.108
All three are required.

161
00:11:17.108 --> 00:11:21.157
Equality and congruence are examples from the table.

162
00:11:21.157 --> 00:11:24.193
Less than and divisibility are not.

163
00:11:24.193 --> 00:11:30.265
When a and b are related by an equivalence relation, we say

164
00:11:30.265 --> 00:11:34.313
they are equivalent with respect to that relation.

165
00:11:34.313 --> 00:11:39.880
This need not mean that they are literally the same object.

166
00:11:39.880 --> 00:11:45.952
For instance, one and four are different integers but are equivalent modulo

167
00:11:45.952 --> 00:11:50.000
three because their difference is divisible by three.

168
00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:54.756
Suppose each CS104 student has one recorded midterm grade.

169
00:11:54.756 --> 00:11:59.511
Relate two students when those grades are the same.

170
00:11:59.511 --> 00:12:04.795
Each student has the same grade as themself, proving reflexivity.

171
00:12:04.795 --> 00:12:10.608
If one student's grade equals another's, the equality reverses, proving symmetry.

172
00:12:10.608 --> 00:12:16.949
If the first and second grades agree and the second and third

173
00:12:16.949 --> 00:12:21.176
agree, the first and third agree, proving transitivity.

174
00:12:21.176 --> 00:12:24.347
Therefore this is an equivalence relation.

175
00:12:24.347 --> 00:12:30.688
The example groups students by the chosen attribute only; it makes no

176
00:12:30.688 --> 00:12:36.500
claim that students sharing a grade are identical in other respects.

177
00:12:36.500 --> 00:12:42.193
Section six point three asks what structure an equivalence relation creates.

178
00:12:42.193 --> 00:12:48.404
For any element a, collect all the elements related to a into

179
00:12:48.404 --> 00:12:49.956
its equivalence class.

180
00:12:49.956 --> 00:12:54.096
We will show that classes cannot overlap partially.

181
00:12:54.096 --> 00:12:58.754
They are either the same set or completely disjoint.

182
00:12:58.754 --> 00:13:04.965
This is what makes them suitable for grouping every element into exactly

183
00:13:04.965 --> 00:13:06.000
one block.

184
00:13:06.000 --> 00:13:11.242
For an equivalence relation R on S, the class of a is

185
00:13:11.242 --> 00:13:16.484
the set of all x in S for which x is related

186
00:13:16.484 --> 00:13:17.358
to a.

187
00:13:17.358 --> 00:13:21.289
The subscript R indicates which relation determines the class.

188
00:13:21.289 --> 00:13:26.532
In the displayed five-element example, look for pairs whose second coordinate is

189
00:13:26.532 --> 00:13:26.968
a.

190
00:13:26.968 --> 00:13:32.211
These are a, a and b, a, so the class of a

191
00:13:32.211 --> 00:13:33.958
contains a and b.

192
00:13:33.958 --> 00:13:39.200
Symmetry would let us inspect the first coordinate instead, but using the

193
00:13:39.200 --> 00:13:41.821
definition directly makes the reasoning explicit.

194
00:13:41.821 --> 00:13:47.063
The class is a set of elements, not a set of ordered

195
00:13:47.063 --> 00:13:47.500
pairs.

196
00:13:47.500 --> 00:13:53.567
The full calculation gives the same class for a and b, namely

197
00:13:53.567 --> 00:13:56.600
the set containing a and b.

198
00:13:56.600 --> 00:14:02.667
The classes of c, d, and e are all the set containing

199
00:14:02.667 --> 00:14:04.689
c, d, and e.

200
00:14:04.689 --> 00:14:09.744
There are five possible representatives but only two distinct classes.

201
00:14:09.744 --> 00:14:15.811
Do not count a class again just because it has a different

202
00:14:15.811 --> 00:14:16.317
representative.

203
00:14:16.317 --> 00:14:20.361
The phrase equivalence class presupposes an equivalence relation.

204
00:14:20.361 --> 00:14:26.428
For an arbitrary relation we can still collect related neighbours, but these

205
00:14:26.428 --> 00:14:32.494
sets need not have the equality-or-disjointness properties that justify the class terminology

206
00:14:32.494 --> 00:14:33.000
here.

207
00:14:33.000 --> 00:14:35.442
Return to the same-grade relation.

208
00:14:35.442 --> 00:14:41.302
The class of David contains every student whose grade equals David's grade.

209
00:14:41.302 --> 00:14:47.163
If David and Mary both have grade A, the class of David

210
00:14:47.163 --> 00:14:53.023
is the set of all students with grade A, and it is

211
00:14:53.023 --> 00:14:55.465
also the class of Mary.

212
00:14:55.465 --> 00:15:01.326
This is equality of two sets, not equality of the two people.

213
00:15:01.326 --> 00:15:07.186
Choosing another representative from a class changes its name but not its

214
00:15:07.186 --> 00:15:07.674
members.

215
00:15:07.674 --> 00:15:13.535
This distinction will be central when we calculate with congruence classes using

216
00:15:13.535 --> 00:15:15.000
different integer representatives.

217
00:15:15.000 --> 00:15:17.389
There are three basic properties.

218
00:15:17.389 --> 00:15:21.689
Every a belongs to its own class, by reflexivity.

219
00:15:21.689 --> 00:15:26.467
Two elements are related exactly when their classes are equal.

220
00:15:26.467 --> 00:15:28.856
Unrelated elements have disjoint classes.

221
00:15:28.856 --> 00:15:34.589
Begin with one direction of the second property: suppose the class of

222
00:15:34.589 --> 00:15:37.456
a equals the class of b.

223
00:15:37.456 --> 00:15:43.189
Since a belongs to its own class, it also belongs to the

224
00:15:43.189 --> 00:15:44.622
class of b.

225
00:15:44.622 --> 00:15:47.967
By definition, a is related to b.

226
00:15:47.967 --> 00:15:53.700
Notice that the starting point a belongs to its class depends on

227
00:15:53.700 --> 00:15:58.000
reflexivity; it is not automatic for an arbitrary relation.

228
00:15:58.000 --> 00:16:02.402
For the reverse direction, suppose a is related to b.

229
00:16:02.402 --> 00:16:06.364
Take an arbitrary x in the class of a.

230
00:16:06.364 --> 00:16:11.647
Then x is related to a, and transitivity with a related to

231
00:16:11.647 --> 00:16:14.288
b gives x related to b.

232
00:16:14.288 --> 00:16:19.571
Thus the class of a is a subset of the class of

233
00:16:19.571 --> 00:16:20.011
b.

234
00:16:20.011 --> 00:16:25.293
Symmetry gives b related to a, so the same argument proves the

235
00:16:25.293 --> 00:16:26.174
reverse inclusion.

236
00:16:26.174 --> 00:16:27.935
The classes are equal.

237
00:16:27.935 --> 00:16:33.217
This proof combines Week Five's two-inclusion method with the exact relation properties

238
00:16:33.217 --> 00:16:38.500
needed to move an arbitrary element from one class to the other.

239
00:16:38.500 --> 00:16:43.500
Now suppose the classes of a and b are disjoint.

240
00:16:43.500 --> 00:16:49.500
If a were related to b, the property just proved would make

241
00:16:49.500 --> 00:16:51.000
their classes equal.

242
00:16:51.000 --> 00:16:56.500
But the class of a is nonempty because it contains a.

243
00:16:56.500 --> 00:17:00.500
Two equal nonempty sets cannot have empty intersection.

244
00:17:00.500 --> 00:17:05.000
This contradiction proves that a and b are unrelated.

245
00:17:05.000 --> 00:17:11.000
The nonempty condition is essential: equality alone would not contradict disjointness if

246
00:17:11.000 --> 00:17:13.000
both sets were empty.

247
00:17:13.000 --> 00:17:17.500
Reflexivity is again doing important work in the background.

248
00:17:17.500 --> 00:17:23.028
For the other direction, prove the contrapositive: if the two classes share

249
00:17:23.028 --> 00:17:26.713
an element, then a is related to b.

250
00:17:26.713 --> 00:17:29.017
Choose x in their intersection.

251
00:17:29.017 --> 00:17:34.084
We have x related to a and x related to b.

252
00:17:34.084 --> 00:17:38.691
Symmetry turns the first relation into a related to x.

253
00:17:38.691 --> 00:17:44.219
Transitivity with x related to b then gives a related to b.

254
00:17:44.219 --> 00:17:49.747
Therefore, if a and b are unrelated, their classes must be disjoint.

255
00:17:49.747 --> 00:17:55.275
Combining the two directions shows that even one shared element forces two

256
00:17:55.275 --> 00:17:58.500
equivalence classes to be the same class.

257
00:17:58.500 --> 00:18:03.529
The distinct equivalence classes form a partition of A.

258
00:18:03.529 --> 00:18:10.235
A partition is a collection of nonempty subsets that cover A and

259
00:18:10.235 --> 00:18:11.912
are pairwise disjoint.

260
00:18:11.912 --> 00:18:18.059
Reflexivity supplies coverage because each element belongs to its own class.

261
00:18:18.059 --> 00:18:21.971
It also guarantees each class is nonempty.

262
00:18:21.971 --> 00:18:26.441
The previous properties guarantee disjointness of distinct classes.

263
00:18:26.441 --> 00:18:33.147
Conversely, any partition defines an equivalence relation by relating elements exactly when

264
00:18:33.147 --> 00:18:36.500
they lie in the same block.

265
00:18:36.500 --> 00:18:43.206
This connection lets us switch between a relation described by pairs and

266
00:18:43.206 --> 00:18:46.000
a grouping described by sets.

267
00:18:46.000 --> 00:18:51.931
How many equivalence relations exist on a set containing a, b, and

268
00:18:51.931 --> 00:18:52.425
c?

269
00:18:52.425 --> 00:18:53.908
Count its partitions.

270
00:18:53.908 --> 00:18:56.874
One partition has three singleton blocks.

271
00:18:56.874 --> 00:19:02.805
Three partitions have a pair and a singleton: a with b, a

272
00:19:02.805 --> 00:19:05.770
with c, or b with c.

273
00:19:05.770 --> 00:19:10.713
The final partition puts all three elements into one block.

274
00:19:10.713 --> 00:19:13.184
That gives five equivalence relations.

275
00:19:13.184 --> 00:19:19.115
For each partition, include every ordered pair whose two entries lie in

276
00:19:19.115 --> 00:19:22.575
the same block, including all diagonal pairs.

277
00:19:22.575 --> 00:19:28.506
Reordering the blocks or changing the representative does not create a new

278
00:19:28.506 --> 00:19:29.000
partition.

279
00:19:29.000 --> 00:19:35.357
Equivalence relations let us work with groups of objects that behave the

280
00:19:35.357 --> 00:19:38.006
same for a chosen purpose.

281
00:19:38.006 --> 00:19:44.363
An infinite set can sometimes be replaced by finitely many classes, as

282
00:19:44.363 --> 00:19:48.601
happens with remainders modulo a fixed positive integer.

283
00:19:48.601 --> 00:19:52.839
This is a possibility, not a universal guarantee.

284
00:19:52.839 --> 00:19:58.667
Equality on the integers, for example, has infinitely many singleton classes.

285
00:19:58.667 --> 00:20:05.024
Also distinguish the number of classes from the number of elements in

286
00:20:05.024 --> 00:20:11.381
a class: congruence modulo three has only three classes, but each class

287
00:20:11.381 --> 00:20:13.500
contains infinitely many integers.

288
00:20:13.500 --> 00:20:19.375
For a positive modulus n, the congruence class of a contains all

289
00:20:19.375 --> 00:20:23.781
integers whose difference from a is divisible by n.

290
00:20:23.781 --> 00:20:27.698
Modulo three there are exactly three distinct classes.

291
00:20:27.698 --> 00:20:32.104
The class of zero contains all multiples of three.

292
00:20:32.104 --> 00:20:37.979
The class of one contains integers such as negative five, negative two,

293
00:20:37.979 --> 00:20:39.938
one, four, and seven.

294
00:20:39.938 --> 00:20:45.813
The class of two contains negative four, negative one, two, five, and

295
00:20:45.813 --> 00:20:46.302
eight.

296
00:20:46.302 --> 00:20:50.708
Every integer belongs to exactly one of these classes.

297
00:20:50.708 --> 00:20:56.583
In particular, negative one is in the class of two because their

298
00:20:56.583 --> 00:21:00.500
difference is negative three, a multiple of three.

299
00:21:00.500 --> 00:21:06.764
Define addition of classes by adding representatives, and multiplication by multiplying representatives.

300
00:21:06.764 --> 00:21:13.027
Modulo five, the class of one plus the class of three is

301
00:21:13.027 --> 00:21:15.115
the class of four.

302
00:21:15.115 --> 00:21:21.379
Replacing the representatives by six and eight gives the class of fourteen,

303
00:21:21.379 --> 00:21:25.033
which is the same class as four.

304
00:21:25.033 --> 00:21:31.297
For multiplication, one times three gives the class of three, while six

305
00:21:31.297 --> 00:21:37.560
times eight gives the class of forty-eight, again the same class as

306
00:21:37.560 --> 00:21:38.082
three.

307
00:21:38.082 --> 00:21:41.736
We must prove this agreement always happens.

308
00:21:41.736 --> 00:21:48.000
A well-defined operation cannot depend on which representative we happened to choose.

309
00:21:48.000 --> 00:21:53.580
Suppose a is congruent to b and c to d modulo n.

310
00:21:53.580 --> 00:21:59.160
Then a minus b and c minus d are multiples of n.

311
00:21:59.160 --> 00:22:04.740
Their sum shows that a plus c is congruent to b plus

312
00:22:04.740 --> 00:22:05.205
d.

313
00:22:05.205 --> 00:22:10.785
For products, write a c minus b d as a times c

314
00:22:10.785 --> 00:22:14.505
minus d, plus d times a minus b.

315
00:22:14.505 --> 00:22:20.085
Each term is divisible by n, so the whole difference is too.

316
00:22:20.085 --> 00:22:23.340
This proves multiplication is independent of representatives.

317
00:22:23.340 --> 00:22:27.990
Repeated multiplication also preserves congruence for every positive integer exponent.

318
00:22:27.990 --> 00:22:33.570
These results justify reducing representatives during a calculation instead of carrying large

319
00:22:33.570 --> 00:22:34.500
integers throughout.

320
00:22:34.500 --> 00:22:40.500
Let S of a positive integer mean the sum of its decimal

321
00:22:40.500 --> 00:22:41.000
digits.

322
00:22:41.000 --> 00:22:47.000
The proposition says the number and its digit sum are congruent modulo

323
00:22:47.000 --> 00:22:47.500
nine.

324
00:22:47.500 --> 00:22:53.500
Consequently, the number is divisible by nine exactly when its digit sum

325
00:22:53.500 --> 00:22:54.000
is.

326
00:22:54.000 --> 00:22:59.500
For seven thousand three hundred nineteen, the digits sum to twenty.

327
00:22:59.500 --> 00:23:05.500
Since twenty is not divisible by nine, neither is the original number.

328
00:23:05.500 --> 00:23:07.500
Both have remainder two.

329
00:23:07.500 --> 00:23:13.500
The statement is stronger than a yes-or-no divisibility test because it identifies

330
00:23:13.500 --> 00:23:15.500
the same residue class.

331
00:23:15.500 --> 00:23:21.500
Next we explain why decimal place values preserve the digit sum modulo

332
00:23:21.500 --> 00:23:22.000
nine.

333
00:23:22.000 --> 00:23:28.109
Expand seven thousand three hundred nineteen as seven times ten cubed, plus

334
00:23:28.109 --> 00:23:33.200
three times ten squared, plus one times ten, plus nine.

335
00:23:33.200 --> 00:23:39.309
Since ten is congruent to one modulo nine, every nonnegative power of

336
00:23:39.309 --> 00:23:41.855
ten is congruent to one.

337
00:23:41.855 --> 00:23:47.964
Using the addition and multiplication rules, the expansion is congruent to seven

338
00:23:47.964 --> 00:23:52.545
plus three plus one plus nine, which is twenty.

339
00:23:52.545 --> 00:23:58.655
The same reasoning works for any decimal expansion, so it proves the

340
00:23:58.655 --> 00:24:02.727
general digit-sum rule rather than just one example.

341
00:24:02.727 --> 00:24:08.836
We have moved from pairs to equivalence classes and then to useful

342
00:24:08.836 --> 00:24:09.345
arithmetic.

343
00:24:09.345 --> 00:24:15.455
In your review, justify each relation property separately and distinguish a representative

344
00:24:15.455 --> 00:24:18.000
from the class it names.
